What is predictive safety in ports and maritime?
Predictive safety in ports uses historical and current operational data to identify patterns associated with elevated risk and help teams prioritise preventive action. It draws on information such as inspections, near misses, permits, equipment findings and corrective actions.

The aim is to recognise developing concerns early enough to investigate and intervene. It does not mean forecasting every incident or knowing exactly when an accident will occur.

A useful output should tell a supervisor what needs attention, which evidence supports the concern and what review is required next.

Prediction, detection and rules: three different jobs
Not every automated safety alert is a prediction. Understanding the difference helps port operators evaluate software more carefully.

Function	Port example	What it tells you
Rules-based alert	A permit has passed its expiry time.	A defined condition has been met.
Detection	A camera identifies a person inside a monitored exclusion zone.	A condition may be present now and needs validation.
Predictive analysis	Repeated location-specific findings and unresolved actions indicate increasing concern.	A pattern may warrant earlier intervention.
All three can be useful. Predictive analysis adds a further perspective; it does not replace mandatory checks or justify waiting when a known hazard requires action.

Which data makes predictive safety useful?
Start with records that describe real work and its controls:

Near misses and observations: what happened, where, during which activity and under what conditions.
Inspection findings: defects, repeat issues and the status of required follow-up.
Permit-to-work records: task, location, duration, authorisation and outstanding verifications.
Corrective actions: ownership, due dates, evidence of completion and effectiveness reviews.
Operational context: relevant activity volumes, equipment use and changes to work arrangements.
Where suitable integrations exist, sensor or camera information may add context. However, more data does not automatically produce better decisions. Records must be timely, consistently labelled and relevant to the question being asked.

For example, “Berth 2,” “B2” and “North Berth” should not describe the same location as three unrelated places. Duplicate reports also need to be recognised so they do not inflate the apparent frequency of a concern.

Three practical applications in port operations
The following scenarios are illustrative workflows, not measured customer outcomes or promises of standard configuration.

1. Identify recurring vehicle–pedestrian concerns
Imagine a terminal receiving several reports about visibility near a warehouse junction. Different workers describe a blind corner, unclear pedestrian routing and temporary obstructions.

AI-assisted text analysis can help group related descriptions for review. Combined with location and activity information, the pattern may help a supervisor decide where to inspect first.

The useful result is a verified response to the concern: checking the arrangement, assigning corrective work and confirming whether the issue recurs.

2. Bring repeated equipment findings into view
An inspection may be completed on time while the same defect continues to appear. A dashboard showing only inspection completion could miss that distinction.

Connecting findings to a consistent equipment identifier helps teams review recurrence, open actions and repair history where available. This can inform an earlier assessment by competent maintenance and operations personnel.

It is important to separate EHS risk analysis from engineering failure prediction. Predicting a component’s failure requires appropriate equipment data and a validated maintenance model; an EHS trend alone does not establish it.

3. Examine overlapping work and control gaps
At a busy berth, maintenance work may continue while cargo activities change. Permit records, work locations and shift handovers can help coordinators understand these overlaps.

NeoEHS Permit to Work software supports digital work-authorisation workflows, including approvals, isolation verification and closure. Reviewing permit information alongside repeated exceptions can help identify work areas requiring closer attention.

A potential conflict still requires competent review of the actual conditions. An automated recommendation should not independently authorise hazardous work.

What should happen after an alert?
An alert is only the beginning. A workable process connects the finding to a responsible person and a documented response:

Show the evidence. Include the relevant records, location, timestamps and reason for the alert.
Assign the review. Route it to someone with the authority and competence to assess the activity.
Check current conditions. Establish whether the concern remains valid and whether immediate controls are needed.
Record the decision. Document the response, owner and follow-up requirements.
Verify effectiveness. Check whether the action addressed the concern and feed the result into future reviews.
Teams should be able to explain why an alert was accepted, escalated or dismissed. That feedback is valuable for improving both the workflow and the analysis.

